window.DIST_CARDS = [{"n":0.1,"ti":"Java Concurrency Refresher","f":"p1-java-concurrency.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Two RequestVote handlers for the same term run on different threads with no lock. What is the dangerous outcome?","o":["The node crashes with a ConcurrentModificationException","Both read votedFor as null and the node votes for both candidates, so two leaders can be elected in one term","The node votes for neither candidate","The term number is incremented twice"],"a":1,"w":"Check-then-act without atomicity: both threads pass the check before either writes. Each candidate counts the vote, which can give both a majority."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Java Concurrency Refresher","f":"p1-java-concurrency.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which of these creates a happens-before edge between thread A's write and thread B's read?","o":["Both threads run on the same CPU core","Thread A writes a plain field, then sleeps for one second","Thread A writes a volatile field, and thread B later reads that same field and sees the value","Thread B calls System.nanoTime() after thread A's write"],"a":2,"w":"A volatile write happens-before every subsequent read of that variable. Sleeping or timing proves nothing to the memory model."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Java Concurrency Refresher","f":"p1-java-concurrency.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A leader replicates each entry to 4 followers and commits at a majority of 5. Why not wait with CompletableFuture.allOf(calls).join() ?","o":["allOf waits for every follower, so one slow or dead follower delays (or blocks) every commit, though only 2 acks are needed","allOf is not thread-safe","allOf returns results in a random order","allOf cannot be combined with timeouts"],"a":0,"w":"Commit latency should be the second-fastest follower here (2 acks plus the leader itself). Waiting for all turns one bad replica into an outage."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Java Concurrency Refresher","f":"p1-java-concurrency.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A stop flag boolean running is set to false by one thread, but the heartbeat loop in another thread keeps running. What's the fix, and why?","o":["Call System.gc() so the threads synchronise","Make the loop sleep longer between heartbeats","Use an AtomicInteger counter instead of a boolean","Declare it volatile: without a happens-before edge, the compiler may keep reading a cached value forever"],"a":3,"w":"Visibility is only guaranteed through happens-before. A volatile write is seen by later volatile reads; a plain field may never be re-read."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Java Concurrency Refresher","f":"p1-java-concurrency.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the main benefit of having one event-loop thread own all of a node's consensus state?","o":["It uses all CPU cores for protocol logic","State changes are serialized without locks and the node becomes deterministic for a given event order, which also enables replay and simulation testing","Network calls no longer need timeouts","It removes the need to persist the vote to disk"],"a":1,"w":"One owner means no data races on protocol state and a single, replayable order of events. Slow I/O still has to happen on other threads."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"RPC & Timeouts","f":"p2-rpc-timeouts.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A client's call to a payment service times out. What does the client know about the charge?","o":["It failed, so it is safe to tell the user to try again","It succeeded, because the server must have received it","Nothing certain: the charge may have happened, may not have, or may still be in progress","It will be rolled back automatically by the network"],"a":2,"w":"A lost request, a crash before or after the charge, a lost reply and a slow server all look the same from the client: no reply in time."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"RPC & Timeouts","f":"p2-rpc-timeouts.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A dependency's latency is p50 = 30 ms, p99 = 120 ms, p99.9 = 400 ms. You can accept about 0.1% of calls timing out falsely. Which timeout fits?","o":["A little above 400 ms","30 ms, the median","120 ms, the p99","30 seconds, to be safe"],"a":0,"w":"Pick the percentile that matches the false-timeout rate you accept (0.1% means p99.9) and add margin. The p99 would time out 1% of calls that would have succeeded."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"RPC & Timeouts","f":"p2-rpc-timeouts.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A request passes through three services, each with its own 1-second timeout on the next call. What is the problem?","o":["Timeouts don't work across more than two services","The innermost service always times out first","gRPC forbids nested timeouts","The user can wait well over a second, and inner services keep working on requests whose callers have already given up"],"a":3,"w":"Per-hop timeouts don't share a budget. A propagated deadline gives each hop only the time left, so the chain gives up together."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"RPC & Timeouts","f":"p2-rpc-timeouts.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why should a deadline be sent between machines as a remaining duration rather than an absolute wall-clock time?","o":["Durations take fewer bytes","The machines' clocks disagree, so an absolute time from one machine means a different instant on another","Absolute times can't be serialized in Protobuf","The receiving machine has no clock"],"a":1,"w":"Clock skew between machines can be milliseconds or seconds. A remaining duration, measured locally on each side's monotonic clock, avoids it."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"RPC & Timeouts","f":"p2-rpc-timeouts.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which reply can a client safely treat as \"definitely not applied\"?","o":["A 504 Gateway Timeout from the load balancer","A connection reset halfway through reading the response","A 400 validation error that the API documents as returned before any work is done","A TimeoutException after 300 ms"],"a":2,"w":"Only an explicit rejection that the server promises happens before acting is a known failure. Gateway errors, resets and timeouts are all unknown outcomes."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Serialization: JSON & Protobuf","f":"p3-serialization.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In Protobuf, what identifies a field in the encoded bytes?","o":["Its name, written before each value","Its field number, combined with the wire type in a tag","Its position in the message","A hash of the field name"],"a":1,"w":"The tag is (field number << 3) | wire type. Names never appear on the wire, which is why renaming is safe and renumbering is not."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Serialization: JSON & Protobuf","f":"p3-serialization.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"During a rolling upgrade, a v1 node receives a message from a v2 node that contains a new field. Which property makes this work?","o":["Backward compatibility","Strong typing","Idempotency","Forward compatibility: old code can read data written by new code"],"a":3,"w":"Old reading new is forward compatibility. New reading old (v2 reading v1 data) is backward compatibility. A rolling upgrade needs both."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Serialization: JSON & Protobuf","f":"p3-serialization.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A team deletes int32 qty = 3 , then later adds int32 discount_pct = 3 . What happens when the new code reads old records?","o":["Old quantities are silently read as discounts, with no error","The parser throws an exception on every old record","The new field reads as 0 for old records","Protobuf detects the reuse at runtime and skips the field"],"a":0,"w":"Same number, same wire type: the bytes are valid, so they are assigned to the new field. Marking 3 as reserved would have prevented the reuse at compile time."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Serialization: JSON & Protobuf","f":"p3-serialization.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How is the integer 150 encoded as a Protobuf varint?","o":["00 96","96","96 01: low 7 bits first with the continuation bit set, then the remaining bits","01 50"],"a":2,"w":"150 = 1·128 + 22. The first byte carries the low 7 bits (22 = 0x16) with the top bit set: 0x96. The second byte carries 1 without the top bit: 0x01."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Serialization: JSON & Protobuf","f":"p3-serialization.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why do Kafka setups that use Avro usually run a schema registry?","o":["Avro messages are encrypted with the schema","Avro writes no field tags, so a reader needs the writer's exact schema to decode the bytes","Kafka cannot store binary data without it","It converts Avro to JSON for consumers"],"a":1,"w":"Avro values are written in schema order with no tags. The message carries a schema ID; the reader fetches that writer schema and resolves it against its own by field name."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Failure Arithmetic: Nines & MTBF","f":"p4-failure-arithmetic.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"About how much downtime per year does 99.99% availability allow?","o":["About 8.8 hours","About 53 minutes","About 5 minutes","About 3.65 days"],"a":1,"w":"0.0001 × 365.25 days × 24 h × 60 min ≈ 52.6 minutes. 8.8 hours is three nines; 5 minutes is five nines."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Failure Arithmetic: Nines & MTBF","f":"p4-failure-arithmetic.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A request must pass through three services in series, each 99.9% available and failing independently. What is the availability of the request path?","o":["99.9%","99.9999999%","About 99.7%","About 97%"],"a":2,"w":"0.999³ ≈ 0.997. Every dependency on the critical path lowers the total."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Failure Arithmetic: Nines & MTBF","f":"p4-failure-arithmetic.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A component fails on average every 1,000 hours and takes 1 hour to recover. What is its availability, and what is usually the cheaper way to add a nine?","o":["About 99.9%; cut recovery time to about 6 minutes with automatic failover","About 99%; buy hardware that fails ten times less often","About 99.99%; nothing further is needed","About 90%; add more monitoring dashboards"],"a":0,"w":"1000 / 1001 ≈ 99.9%. A tenfold faster recovery (MTTR 6 min) gives about 99.99%, and automation is usually easier than tenfold rarer failures."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Failure Arithmetic: Nines & MTBF","f":"p4-failure-arithmetic.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why does a 4-node consensus cluster tolerate no more failures than a 3-node one?","o":["Even numbers of nodes cannot elect a leader","The fourth node is always a read-only replica","Raft limits clusters to odd sizes","A majority of 4 is 3, so losing 2 nodes already blocks progress, the same as 3 nodes losing 2"],"a":3,"w":"Fault tolerance is n minus the majority: 3 − 2 = 1 and 4 − 3 = 1. Five nodes (majority 3) tolerate two failures."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Failure Arithmetic: Nines & MTBF","f":"p4-failure-arithmetic.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Three database replicas are spread over three racks in one availability zone. Which event does the replica count NOT protect against?","o":["One server's disk failing","A zone-wide power or network failure, or a bad config pushed to all replicas at once","One rack's switch failing","A kernel panic on one server"],"a":1,"w":"Replicas only help against failures they don't share. A zone outage or a fleet-wide config change is a common-mode failure that hits every replica together."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Clocks on One Machine","f":"p5-clocks-on-one-machine.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which Java call should a heartbeat-based failure detector use to measure time since the last heartbeat?","o":["System.currentTimeMillis()","Instant.now()","System.nanoTime()","LocalDateTime.now()"],"a":2,"w":"Elapsed time needs the monotonic clock. The other three read the wall clock, which can jump when NTP or an operator changes it."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Clocks on One Machine","f":"p5-clocks-on-one-machine.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"NTP finds the local clock 50 ms fast. What does a typical NTP daemon do?","o":["Slews: runs the clock slightly slow until the offset is gone, without any jump","Steps the clock back 50 ms immediately","Reboots the machine","Nothing; 50 ms is within tolerance"],"a":0,"w":"Small offsets are slewed (at most 500 ppm). ntpd steps only above 128 ms by default; chrony steps only when configured to."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Clocks on One Machine","f":"p5-clocks-on-one-machine.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why did some programs compute negative durations during the leap second of 31 December 2016?","o":["The monotonic clock ran backwards","NTP servers sent invalid packets","Java's Duration class can't represent leap seconds","They subtracted wall-clock readings, and the wall clock repeated a second"],"a":3,"w":"Unix time has no 23:59:60, so most systems repeat 23:59:59. A duration measured across that repeat on the wall clock can come out negative."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Clocks on One Machine","f":"p5-clocks-on-one-machine.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How should two System.nanoTime() readings, a and b, be compared to ask \"is b later than a\"?","o":["b > a","b - a > 0","Convert both to Instant first","They can't be compared at all"],"a":1,"w":"nanoTime values can be negative and can wrap, so the Javadoc says to subtract and compare the difference with zero. Readings from different JVMs are not comparable at all."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Clocks on One Machine","f":"p5-clocks-on-one-machine.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A leader holds a lease measured on the wall clock. NTP steps its clock back 2 seconds just before the lease ends. What happens?","o":["The lease ends 2 seconds early","Nothing; leases ignore clock changes","The leader believes its lease is valid for 2 seconds longer than it really is, and may act as leader after a new one exists","The lease is renewed automatically"],"a":2,"w":"Stepping back makes the wall clock read earlier, so the lease's end appears further away. Lease timers belong on the monotonic clock."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Hashing for Distribution","f":"p6-hashing.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A cache cluster using hash mod N grows from 4 to 5 servers. Roughly what fraction of keys now map to a different server?","o":["20%","80%","25%","0%: existing keys stay put"],"a":1,"w":"A key stays only if h mod 4 equals h mod 5, about 1 key in 5. So about 4/5 = 80% move. Consistent hashing would move about 20%."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Hashing for Distribution","f":"p6-hashing.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why is Math.abs(hash) % n a bug?","o":["It is slower than floorMod","It gives a different answer on different JVMs","It can't handle n larger than 1,000","Math.abs(Integer.MIN_VALUE) is negative, so a rare key gets a negative shard number"],"a":3,"w":"Integer.MIN_VALUE has no positive counterpart in 32-bit two's complement. Math.floorMod(h, n) always returns a value in [0, n)."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Hashing for Distribution","f":"p6-hashing.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why can't Object.hashCode() be used to choose which server stores a key?","o":["For classes that don't override it, the value is not defined by the object's contents and differs between JVM runs and machines","It returns a 64-bit value","It is too slow","It always returns 0 for strings"],"a":0,"w":"Placement must be computed identically by every client and server. Identity hash codes are per-process. Hash the key's bytes with a fixed function."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Hashing for Distribution","f":"p6-hashing.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"With String.hashCode and N = 31 servers, the keys order-000001 to order-001000 land on only 10 servers. Why?","o":["31 is a prime number, which hash tables avoid","The keys are too long for a 32-bit hash","hashCode is a polynomial in 31; the shared prefix adds the same constant to every key, so modulo 31 only the last character varies, and it is one of ten digits","Java caches hash codes for short strings"],"a":2,"w":"The prefix contributes one constant to all the keys, and every other digit term s[i]·31^k with k ≥ 1 is a multiple of 31. So the remainder mod 31 is set by the last character. A well-mixed hash removes such structure."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Hashing for Distribution","f":"p6-hashing.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A perfectly uniform hash spreads keys evenly, yet one server is at 100% CPU while others idle. What is the most likely cause?","o":["The hash function has a bias","A hot key: one key receives a large share of requests, and all of them must go to the server that owns it","The number of servers is even","Clock skew between servers"],"a":1,"w":"Hashing balances the number of keys, not the traffic per key. Hot keys need caching, splitting into sub-keys, or special routing."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"How to Read a Systems Paper","f":"p7-reading-papers.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What should a first pass over a systems paper cover?","o":["Title, abstract, introduction, headings and conclusion, in about ten minutes","Every proof, to check correctness","The evaluation section only","The full paper, front to back, taking notes on every paragraph"],"a":0,"w":"The first pass is a quick skim to decide whether to read further and to find the category, context, contributions and model."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"How to Read a Systems Paper","f":"p7-reading-papers.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Raft's safety does not depend on timing, but its availability does. What does that mean?","o":["Raft only works on networks with bounded delays","Raft never needs timeouts","However slow or unpredictable the network is, Raft never commits conflicting entries; it may just fail to make progress until delays settle","Raft's clocks must be synchronised with NTP"],"a":2,"w":"Safety (nothing bad happens) holds under any delays. Liveness (progress) needs delays comfortably below the election timeout."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"How to Read a Systems Paper","f":"p7-reading-papers.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"\"The system is designed to provide 99.99% availability.\" Which kind of claim is this?","o":["Evidence","Mechanism","Assumption","A goal: it says what the designers aimed for, not that it was achieved"],"a":3,"w":"Look for evidence (measurements over a stated period) before treating a design target as a result."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"How to Read a Systems Paper","f":"p7-reading-papers.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why read a proof mainly for its statement?","o":["Proofs in systems papers are usually wrong","The statement is the invariant the implementation must preserve, and you can sanity-check it on a small example","Proofs can't be understood without formal training","The statement is shorter"],"a":1,"w":"Knowing the invariant (for example Raft's Leader Completeness) tells you what any change to the code must not break."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"How to Read a Systems Paper","f":"p7-reading-papers.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A team builds on the Dynamo paper's design. What is the main risk of relying on the paper alone?","o":["Real systems depart from their papers over time, and the paper says nothing about later changes, defaults, bugs or how the system behaved in production","The Dynamo paper was never peer-reviewed","Papers are copyrighted and can't be implemented","The paper's algorithms are too slow for production"],"a":0,"w":"DynamoDB, for example, differs substantially from the 2007 design. Read documentation, release notes, Jepsen analyses and postmortems as well."}];
